L = {}
L["jonathan"] = """After David defeated Goliath, King Saul brought him to the palace. David was still a teenager, a shepherd boy from Bethlehem. As soon as he finished speaking with Saul, something happened that would change his life. The Bible says that the soul of Jonathan was knit to the soul of David, and Jonathan loved him as himself.

Jonathan was Saul's oldest son. He was a prince, the heir to the throne. He was also a brave soldier. Earlier in the book of First Samuel, he and his armor bearer had climbed a cliff and attacked a whole Philistine outpost, because he trusted that nothing could keep the Lord from saving, whether by many or by few. He had every reason to see David as a rival. A shepherd boy had just done what no one in Saul's army, including Jonathan, had been willing to do.

But Jonathan didn't see a rival. He saw a friend. He made a covenant with David because he loved him as himself. Jonathan took off the robe he was wearing and gave it to David, along with his tunic, his sword, his bow, and his belt. This was no small gift. A prince's robe and weapons were a sign of his position. In giving them away, Jonathan was in a sense handing over his future as king.

David was successful in everything Saul sent him to do, and Saul gave him a high rank in the army. But as the army came home, the women came out from all the towns of Israel to meet King Saul with singing and dancing, with joyful songs and with timbrels and lutes. And they sang, Saul has slain his thousands, and David his tens of thousands. Saul was very angry. This refrain displeased him greatly. What more can he get but the kingdom? And from that time on, Saul kept a jealous eye on David.

The next day, an evil spirit came on Saul, and David was playing the lyre as he usually did. Saul had a spear in his hand and he hurled it, saying to himself, I'll pin David to the wall. But David eluded him twice. Saul was afraid of David, because the Lord was with David but had departed from Saul.

Jonathan became David's protector. When Saul told his servants and Jonathan to kill David, Jonathan warned David. Jonathan spoke up for David to his father. He said, let not the king do wrong to his servant David. He has not wronged you, and what he has done has benefited you greatly. He took his life in his hands when he killed the Philistine. Saul listened and swore, as surely as the Lord lives, David will not be put to death.

But the jealousy came back. David ran away. In secret, Jonathan and David met at a field. Jonathan promised to find out what his father really planned. They agreed on a signal with arrows. Jonathan shot three arrows and told his servant boy, isn't the arrow beyond you? Hurry! That was the sign that David had to flee. Then the two friends kissed each other and wept together, though David wept the most.

Jonathan said to David, go in peace, for we have sworn friendship with each other in the name of the Lord, saying, the Lord is witness between you and me, and between your descendants and my descendants forever.

Why does this matter? Jonathan chose to love David, even when it cost him the throne. He knew that God had chosen David to be king. He knew the crown that should have been his would go to David. He gave up his claim willingly. In a world where people fight for power, Jonathan is a picture of someone who put God's plan, and a friend, ahead of his own ambition.

It also shows what friendship can be. Jonathan didn't just say nice words. He risked his life to save David. He was loyal when it cost him. Proverbs says a friend loves at all times, and a brother is born for a time of adversity.

What happened after? Jonathan died with his father in a battle on Mount Gilboa. David wept for him and wrote a song. I grieve for you, Jonathan my brother. You were very dear to me. Your love for me was wonderful, more wonderful than that of women. Later, David kept his promise. He found Jonathan's son Mephibosheth, who had been injured as a child, and invited him to eat at the king's table for the rest of his life.

What does this mean for us today? Think about who the Jonathans in your life are. Who has given you an encouragement, or a warning, or a place to belong? And who needs you to be that for them?

It also asks us a hard question. Can we cheer for someone who is getting what we wanted? Jonathan did that. His joy in David's success was greater than his own desire for the throne. That kind of generosity is rare, and it is how Jesus asks us to love.

That is why this story is so important. It shows a friendship built on shared trust in God. Jonathan and David swore their love in the name of the Lord, and that is what made it last. Jesus said, greater love has no one than this, to lay down one's life for one's friends."""

L["spares-saul"] = """David was running for his life. King Saul was jealous, and he had taken three thousand of his best soldiers to hunt David down in the wilderness near En Gedi, a desert oasis with cliffs and caves along the Dead Sea. David and his six hundred men were hiding.

Saul came to a sheep pen along the way. There was a cave there, and he went in to relieve himself. It happened that David and his men were far back in that very cave. They whispered to David, this is the day the Lord spoke of when he said to you, I will give your enemy into your hands for you to deal with as you wish. David crept up unnoticed and cut off a corner of Saul's robe.

Afterward, David was conscience-stricken for having cut off a corner of his robe. He said to his men, the Lord forbid that I should do such a thing to my master, the Lord's anointed, or lay my hand on him. He is the anointed of the Lord. With these words David sharply rebuked his men and did not allow them to attack Saul. And Saul left the cave and went on his way.

Think about what David had every reason to do. Saul had tried to kill him with a spear, had hunted him for years, and had even murdered the priests of Nob for helping him. David had already been anointed by the prophet Samuel as the next king. His men were telling him that God had handed Saul over. But David said no. He would not take the throne by force. He would wait for God to give it to him.

After Saul had left the cave and gone on his way, David went out and called out to him, my lord the king! When Saul looked behind him, David bowed down and prostrated himself with his face to the ground. He said, why do you listen when people say, David is bent on harming you? This day you have seen with your own eyes how the Lord delivered you into my hands in the cave. Some urged me to kill you, but I spared you. I said, I will not lay my hand on my lord, because he is the Lord's anointed.

Then David held up the piece of Saul's robe. See, my father, look at this piece of your robe in my hand! I cut off the corner of your robe but did not kill you. See that there is nothing in my hands to indicate that I am guilty of wrongdoing or rebellion. I have not wronged you, but you are hunting me down to take my life. May the Lord judge between you and me. And may the Lord avenge the wrongs you have done to me, but my hand will not touch you.

Saul wept aloud. He said, you are more righteous than I. You have treated me well, but I have treated you badly. You have just now told me about the good you did to me. The Lord delivered me into your hands, but you did not kill me. When a man finds his enemy, does he let him get away unharmed? May the Lord reward you well for the way you treated me today. Saul even admitted that David would be king. Swear to me that you will not kill off my descendants. David gave his oath.

Then Saul returned home, but David and his men went up to the stronghold. Sadly, this was not the end of Saul's pursuit. He would turn on David again. But David had shown who he was.

Why does this matter? David had the power to end his troubles in one stroke. He chose not to. He understood that being right does not give us the right to take revenge. He left justice to God. Later, David had the chance a second time, when Saul was asleep in his camp, and again he spared him. Paul would later write, do not take revenge, my dear friends, but leave room for God's wrath.

It also shows how a leader should treat authority. Saul was not a good king, and he had been rejected by God. But David respected the office. He waited for God to put him on the throne, not his own hand.

What happened after? Saul died in battle on Mount Gilboa, and David became king. He didn't gain the throne through violence against his master, and the people knew it. That helped him unite the nation.

What does this mean for us today? We will all have people who wrong us, and sometimes we will have the chance to get even. This story teaches us to pause and ask, what would honor God here? Mercy is not weakness. It takes more strength to spare an enemy than to hurt him.

It also tells us to wait. David was promised a throne, and he could have taken it. He waited years. Often, God gives us things in his time, and the waiting is part of what shapes us.

That is why this story is so important. It is a preview of the way of Jesus. He said, love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you. David, in a dark cave, did it first. And one day, a descendant of David, hanging on a cross, would say, Father, forgive them."""

L["absalom"] = """David was a great king, but his family was a mess. His troubles started with his own sin. He committed adultery with Bathsheba and had her husband Uriah killed. The prophet Nathan told him that because of this, the sword would never depart from his house. And it didn't. His oldest son Amnon assaulted his half-sister Tamar. Her brother Absalom hated Amnon for it and, after two years, had him killed. Absalom fled and stayed away for three years. David longed for him, and eventually brought him home. But he refused to see Absalom for two more years.

Absalom was handsome, the Bible says. There was no one in all Israel so highly praised for his good looks. When he cut his hair, it weighed about five pounds. He began to stand at the city gate every morning and greet people who came with a complaint. If only I were appointed judge in the land, he would say. Then everyone who has a complaint could come to me and I would see that they receive justice. And whenever anyone approached him to bow down, he would reach out, take hold of him, and kiss him. In this way, the Bible says, Absalom stole the hearts of the people of Israel.

After four years, he went to Hebron and had himself declared king. The conspiracy gained strength, and the number of people who supported him kept growing. A messenger came to David and said, the hearts of the people of Israel are with Absalom. David left Jerusalem on foot, weeping, with his head covered and his feet bare. He had to flee from his own son.

The two armies met in the forest of Ephraim. David stayed behind. As the army marched out, the king gave an order to Joab and the other commanders. Be gentle with the young man Absalom for my sake. Everyone heard the king giving orders about Absalom. It is a startling sentence. His son was trying to take his throne, and David's thought was for him.

The battle was fought, and the rebels were defeated. Absalom happened to meet some of David's men. He was riding his mule, and as it went under the thick branches of a large oak, his hair got caught in the tree. He was left hanging in midair, while the mule he was riding kept on going. When one of the men reported it to Joab, Joab, who was not so gentle, took three javelins and plunged them into Absalom's heart. Ten of his armor bearers then surrounded Absalom and struck him and killed him.

Joab then sounded the trumpet, and the troops stopped. They took Absalom's body, threw it into a big pit in the forest, and piled a large heap of rocks over it.

Meanwhile, David was sitting between the inner and outer gates of the city. A messenger arrived. Good news for my lord the king! The Lord has delivered you today from all who rose up against you. David asked only one thing. Is the young man Absalom safe? The man said, may the enemies of my lord the king and all who rise up to harm you be like that young man. The king was shaken. He went up to the room over the gateway and wept. As he went, he said, O my son Absalom! My son, my son Absalom! If only I had died instead of you, O Absalom, my son, my son!

Why does this matter? It shows that even great people suffer from their mistakes, and that sin has long consequences. David was forgiven by God, but the effects of what he'd done kept playing out in his family. It also shows the heart of a father. David was a king who had won a victory, and yet he grieved for a son who tried to kill him.

It points us to the heart of God. The way David loved a rebellious son is the way God loves us. And the words, if only I had died instead of you, were later made real. Jesus did die in the place of those who rebelled against him.

What happened after? Joab came to David and told him bluntly to pull himself together, since the army was ashamed. David went back to the gate and sat down. He returned to Jerusalem as king. But the cost was heavy, and he spent the rest of his reign dealing with the aftermath.

What does this mean for us today? If you are a parent grieving a child who has gone the wrong way, you are not alone. David's cry is in the Bible so that people who feel this pain know that God understands. He saw it in his own Son's case.

It also warns us about quiet ambition. Absalom didn't begin with a sword. He began at the gate, with a smile and a kiss and a promise. Be careful about people who steal hearts, and about stealing them yourself.

That is why this story is so important. It tells the truth about love and failure in the same family. It shows a father who could not save his son, and it points to the Father who could. When we were rebels, he did not say, be gentle, and then look away. He gave his own Son so we could live."""

L["solomon-judgment"] = """David was dead. His son Solomon became king of Israel, but he was young, probably in his twenties, and he knew it. Early in his reign, he went to Gibeon to offer sacrifices. That night, the Lord appeared to him in a dream. God said, ask for whatever you want me to give you.

Think about that. Anything. Solomon could have asked for riches, a long life, or the death of his enemies. Instead, he answered with a humble prayer. You have shown great kindness to your servant, my father David. Now, Lord my God, you have made your servant king in place of my father David. But I am only a little child and do not know how to carry out my duties. Your servant is here among the people you have chosen, a great people, too numerous to count. So give your servant a discerning heart to govern your people and to distinguish between right and wrong. For who is able to govern this great people of yours?

The Lord was pleased that Solomon had asked for this. God said, since you have asked for this and not for long life or wealth for yourself, nor have asked for the death of your enemies, I will do what you have asked. I will give you a wise and discerning heart, so that there will never have been anyone like you, nor will there ever be. Moreover, I will give you what you have not asked for, both wealth and honor. Solomon woke up and realized it had been a dream. He returned to Jerusalem and offered sacrifices.

Not long after, two women came to the king. They lived in the same house, and each had given birth to a baby, three days apart. One night, one of the babies died. The woman whose child had died got up in the night and took the living child from the other woman's side, and put her dead child there. In the morning, the second woman saw the dead baby and realized it wasn't hers.

They were both claiming the living child. One said, no! The dead one is yours. The living one is mine. The other said, no! The living one is mine. The dead one is yours. They argued in front of the king. There were no witnesses, no records, and no tests. It was one woman's word against another's.

The king said, bring me a sword. Then he said, cut the living child in two and give half to one and half to the other. The woman whose son was alive was deeply moved out of love for her son. She said, please, my lord, give her the living baby! Don't kill him! But the other said, neither I nor you shall have him. Cut him in two!

Then the king gave his ruling. Give the living baby to the first woman. Do not kill him. She is his mother. When all Israel heard the verdict the king had given, they held the king in awe, because they saw that he had wisdom from God to administer justice.

Why does this matter? Solomon's wisdom didn't come from his intelligence. It was a gift that he asked for. He knew what he lacked, and he asked God. James says, if any of you lacks wisdom, you should ask God, who gives generously to all without finding fault, and it will be given to you.

It also shows what true love looks like. The real mother was willing to lose her child, so he could live. She cared more about the baby's life than about winning. This is the same kind of love God shows. In a sense, it pictures the love of a Father who gives up his Son.

What happened after? Solomon built the temple in Jerusalem and ruled in peace and wealth. His fame went far and wide, as we see in the story of the Queen of Sheba. But in his old age, he married many foreign women and allowed their gods into his heart. He started well and ended poorly, and his kingdom was split after his death.

What does this mean for us today? When we are asked what we want most, what do we say? Solomon asked for wisdom, and he got that plus everything else. Jesus said something similar. Seek first his kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be given to you as well.

It also encourages us. You may feel too young, too new, or too unprepared for what you are facing. Solomon said he was a little child. God didn't tell him to be more confident. He gave him what he needed. And God gives wisdom to those who ask.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that real wisdom is a gift from God, and it begins with admitting we don't know. Solomon's wisdom was great, but the Bible says one greater than Solomon has come. That is Jesus."""

L["sheba"] = """Solomon was now the most famous king in the world. God had given him wisdom, wealth, and peace. He had built the temple in Jerusalem, a magnificent house for the Lord, and a palace for himself. His fame went far beyond Israel. Visitors from many nations came to hear his wisdom. One of them traveled from a distant land called Sheba, probably in southern Arabia, in what is today Yemen, though some think it may have been Ethiopia. The Bible says the queen of Sheba heard about the fame of Solomon and his relation to the name of the Lord, and she came to test him with hard questions.

It was a long trip, over a thousand miles, across the desert by camel. She came to Jerusalem with a very great caravan, with camels carrying spices, large quantities of gold, and precious stones. This was more than a courtesy call. She was a queen, and a smart one. She wanted to see for herself. She did not believe what she had been told.

When she came to Solomon, she talked with him about all that she had on her mind. Solomon answered all her questions. Nothing was too hard for the king to explain to her. When the queen of Sheba saw all the wisdom of Solomon and the palace he had built, the food on his table, the seating of his officials, the attending servants in their robes, his cupbearers, and the burnt offerings he made at the temple of the Lord, she was overwhelmed.

She said to the king, the report I heard in my own country about your achievements and your wisdom is true. But I did not believe these things until I came and saw with my own eyes. Indeed, not even half was told me. In wisdom and wealth you have far exceeded the report I heard. How happy your people must be! How happy your officials, who continually stand before you and hear your wisdom! Praise be to the Lord your God, who has delighted in you and placed you on the throne of Israel. Because of the Lord's eternal love for Israel, he has made you king to maintain justice and righteousness.

Notice what she said at the end. She didn't just praise Solomon. She praised the Lord his God. A foreign queen, from a land with its own gods, was led by Solomon's wisdom to the God behind it. That was what Israel was meant to do, to show the world what it looks like to follow the true God.

She gave the king 120 talents of gold, large quantities of spices, and precious stones. Never again were so many spices brought in as the queen of Sheba gave to King Solomon. And King Solomon gave the queen of Sheba all she desired and asked for, in addition to what he had given her out of his royal bounty. Then she left and returned with her retinue to her own country.

Why does this matter? It shows what God intended for Israel. They were meant to be a light to the nations, so that when outsiders saw how God blessed his people, they would want to know him. The queen of Sheba's visit is an early picture of what Isaiah promised, that nations would come to Zion's light.

It is also remembered by Jesus. He said, the Queen of the South will rise at the judgment with this generation and condemn it, for she came from the ends of the earth to listen to Solomon's wisdom, and now something greater than Solomon is here. Jesus was telling the people around him that they had greater evidence, and still didn't come to him. She traveled a thousand miles for the wisdom of a king. They had the wisdom of God standing in front of them.

What happened after? Solomon's reign continued for forty years. Tradition in Ethiopia says that the queen had a son by Solomon, who founded the royal line there. The Bible doesn't say this, but tradition has said so for centuries. What the Bible does tell us is that Solomon's heart later drifted, and his kingdom divided after his death.

What does this mean for us today? She was willing to go the distance to find the truth. She asked hard questions and didn't just accept what she'd heard. God welcomes honest questions. If you are searching for answers about God, don't be afraid to bring your hardest ones.

And once she saw, she gave. She didn't leave empty-handed or unchanged. When we really meet the wisdom of God, it tends to move us to respond with generosity and praise.

That is why this story is so important. It says that the God of Israel is for everyone, from every nation, and that those who seek him will find him. And it points to Jesus, who is greater than Solomon, and who invites everyone who is thirsty to come and see."""
